  "query_preview": "/TUESDAY NIGHT THROUGH SUNDAY/...\nIssued at 308 AM CDT Mon Aug 24 2026\n\nA frontal boundary will sag south stalling across the southern \npanhandle east southeast along the Kansas/Nebraska border. \nEasterly flow in the low levels will lead to highs in the lower \nto middle 80s Wednesday. Thunderstorms look to develop Wednesday\nafternoon across the panhandle and southeastern Wyoming INVOF \nthe front. This activity should track southeast with mid level \nnorthwesterly winds. ATTM, the EC, NAM12 and GFS deterministic \nsolns have this favorable forcing over the eastern panhandle and\nfar SW Nebraska. This is also in agreement with the latest NBM \nforecast. On Thursday, an upper level trough, will enter \nsouthwestern Canada suppressing the ridge across the \nintermountain west and forcing warmer air east onto the high \nplains. Favorable mid level warm air advection Thursday night \nand a warm front lifting northeast across the forecast area, \nwill lead to an increased threat for thunderstorms Thursday \nnight. By Friday, the upper level trough will track east and \nsoutheast into the Pacific NW. This will flatten the ridge \nfurther resulting in westerly mid level winds for Friday into \nSunday. This flattening of the ridge will also drive warm air \nfurther east into the remainder of the forecast area. By \nSaturday and Sunday, highs will reach in the upper 80s in the \neast with lower to middle 90s in the west and southwest. Weak \ndisturbances will cross the central Rockies Friday through \nSunday with precipitation chances continuing Friday night and \nSaturday night.",
